  "Number": 24,
  "Question": "What is the correct ordering of the three numbers, \\(10^8,\\) \\(5^{12},\\) and \\(2^{24}?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2^{24} \\lt 10^8 \\lt 5^{12}\\)",
    "B": "\\( 2^{24} \\lt 5^{12} \\lt 10^8 \\)",
    "C": "\\( 5^{12} \\lt 2^{24} \\lt 10^8 \\)",
    "D": "\\( 10^8 \\lt 5^{12} \\lt 2^{24} \\)",
    "E": "\\( 10^8 \\lt 2^{24} \\lt 5^{12} \\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "The exponents \\(8\\), \\(12\\), and \\(24\\) are all multiples of \\(4\\). Grouping the repeated factors into four equal groups turns the problem into a comparison of much smaller numbers:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2^{24}&=(2^6)^4=64^4,\\\\\n10^8&=(10^2)^4=100^4,\\\\\n5^{12}&=(5^3)^4=125^4.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nWe have \\(64<100<125\\). Replacing any positive factor by a larger positive factor increases the product, so the fourth powers have the same order. Therefore\n\\[\n\\boxed{2^{24}<10^8<5^{12}},\n\\]\nwhich is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
